      "t": "Хорошо. Нам то, что у него.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шоколадный ветер три раза.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спагетти и равиоли с клубникой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Утка два раза.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Принято!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Итак, ваш заказ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So, here is your order.' Notice how Russian expresses 'so' and 'order' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А это что?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what is that?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'what'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Честно говоря, ерунда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Honestly speaking, nonsense.' Max anchors the line on 'honestly', while 'nonsense' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А вы попробуйте. Это очень вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Try it. It is very delicious.' Max chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'deliciously' to keep a polite distance.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Высокая кухня от повара из Парижа.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Haute cuisine from a chef from Paris.' The meaning hinges on 'high' working together with 'Paris' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А давай попробуем!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's try it!' In Russian, the idea of 'and' flows naturally into 'will try' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Хорошо. Нам то, что у него.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good. Give us what he is having.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'well' and finishing on 'he'. Max answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course!' Notice how Russian expresses 'of course' and 'of course' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шоколадный ветер три раза.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chocolate wind three times.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'chocolate' before landing on 'times'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Спагетти и равиоли с клубникой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Spaghetti and ravioli with strawberry.' Max brings out the contrast between 'spaghetti' and 'and' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Утка два раза.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Duck twice.' Max anchors the line on 'duck', while 'times' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Принято!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Copy that!' The grammar packs 'accepted' and 'accepted' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
